solution:
Label each point of $S$ by its clockwise arc in degrees from $A$. The points are the multiples of $30$ and $72$, and these coincide only at $0$ and $360$, which are both $A$. Every step moves to the next multiple of $30$ or the next multiple of $72$, and no step can pass $360$. So admissible polygons correspond exactly to routes from $0$ to $360$. Each arc is at most $72$, so every route has at least $5$ arcs and gives a genuine polygon.

The possible steps, with arc lengths in brackets, are:
$0\to30\ (30),\ 0\to72\ (72)$;
$30\to60\ (30),\ 30\to72\ (42)$;
$60\to90\ (30),\ 60\to72\ (12)$;
$72\to90\ (18),\ 72\to144\ (72)$;
$90\to120\ (30),\ 90\to144\ (54)$;
$120\to150\ (30),\ 120\to144\ (24)$;
$144\to150\ (6),\ 144\to216\ (72)$;
$150\to180\ (30),\ 150\to216\ (66)$;
$180\to210\ (30),\ 180\to216\ (36)$;
$210\to240\ (30),\ 210\to216\ (6)$;
$216\to240\ (24),\ 216\to288\ (72)$;
$240\to270\ (30),\ 240\to288\ (48)$;
$270\to300\ (30),\ 270\to288\ (18)$;
$288\to300\ (12),\ 288\to360\ (72)$;
$300\to330\ (30),\ 300\to360\ (60)$;
$330\to360\ (30)$.

Suppose the arcs on either side of a vertex $P$ are $a$ and $b$. The interior angle at $P$ is an inscribed angle standing on the remaining arc $360^\circ-a-b$, so it equals $\frac{360^\circ-a-b}{2}$. Hence the angle is at least $120^\circ$ exactly when $a+b\le120^\circ$.

Now we find the forbidden adjacent pairs.
If neither arc is $72$, the sum is at most $120$. The only arc above $60$ other than $72$ is the $66$ arc $150\to216$. It is entered by an arc of $6$ or $30$, and if it is left by a non-$72$ arc, that arc is $24$.
If a $72$ arc is followed by a non-$72$ arc, that second arc leaves a pentagon vertex and has length at most $30$, so the sum is at most $102$.
This leaves a $72$ arc leaving a pentagon vertex, with entering arcs as follows:
at $72$: entering $72, 42, 12$, of which only $72$ fails;
at $144$: entering $72, 54, 24$, of which $72$ and $54$ fail;
at $216$: entering $72, 66, 36, 6$, of which $72$ and $66$ fail;
at $288$: entering $72, 48, 18$, of which only $72$ fails, since $48+72=120$ is allowed;
at $A$: last arc $72, 60, 30$, with first arc $72$, of which $72$ and $60$ fail.

So at each pentagon vertex, call a route <b>free</b> if it may leave by a $72$ arc and <b>blocked</b> otherwise. Let $f(v)$ be the number of valid routes reaching a $12$-gon point $v$. At a pentagon vertex, record the pair (free, blocked). Only free routes may use a $72$ arc out, and that arc makes the route blocked at the next pentagon vertex.

<b>Case 1: the first arc is $30$.</b> A first arc of $30$ adds to any last arc without exceeding $120$, so the closing pair at $A$ imposes no restriction.
$f(30)=f(60)=1$.
At $72$: $(2,0)$, from $30$ and $60$.
$f(90)=1+2=3$ and $f(120)=3$.
At $144$: free $3$ from $120$; blocked $2$ from $72$ plus $3$ from $90$, giving $(3,5)$.
$f(150)=3+8=11$ and $f(180)=f(210)=11$.
At $216$: free $11+11=22$ from $180$ and $210$; blocked $3$ from $144$ plus $11$ from $150$, giving $(22,14)$.
$f(240)=11+36=47$ and $f(270)=47$.
At $288$: free $47+47=94$; blocked $22$ from $216$, giving $(94,22)$.
$f(300)=47+116=163$ and $f(330)=163$.
Routes reach $360$ from $288$ by free routes only ($94$), from $300$ ($163$) and from $330$ ($163$), giving $420$ routes.

<b>Case 2: the first arc is $72$.</b> The last arc must then be $30$, so the route must end $330\to360$. Also, $72$ is reached blocked, as $(0,1)$, and the points $30$ and $60$ are not visited.
$f(90)=f(120)=1$.
At $144$: $(1,1)$, from $120$ and $90$ respectively.
$f(150)=1+2=3$ and $f(180)=f(210)=3$.
At $216$: free $3+3=6$; blocked $1$ from $144$ plus $3$ from $150$, giving $(6,4)$.
$f(240)=3+10=13$ and $f(270)=13$.
At $288$: free $13+13=26$; blocked $6$, giving $(26,6)$.
$f(300)=13+32=45$ and $f(330)=45$.
All of these close with the arc $330\to360$, giving $45$ routes.

The total is $420+45=465$, so the answer is $465$.

<b>Why this works.</b> <code>recursion</code>, <code>inscribed-angle-theorem</code>, <code>casework</code>: the angle condition only restricts adjacent arcs, so a position-by-position count needs to remember just the last arc's effect, plus a split on the first arc to close the cycle at $A$.
